US English speaker learning Polish, A1–A2, saying which household chores they do. When the tip is the present-tense endings of common verbs like "sprzątać" or "gotować" ("sprzątam", "sprzątasz", "sprząta"), a pattern table shows one verb's endings for each person and has the learner complete another verb of the same group; every form in it is correct Polish, and each blank's right ending is the longest choice its answer ends with. A tip about something else has no table. Sentences use chores a learner really does ("Sprzątam kuchnię", "Robię pranie") with the accusative where it belongs.